Overlay patch 0052 (VR R9 part B): A SENSE CONTROLLER IS NOT AN SDL GAMEPAD.

the user, on 1.0.1.12: "The right joystick is STILL sometimes triggering C
buttons! Stop this! It should ONLY trigger C buttons when you're holding
ocarina."

THE CAUSE, and none of it is in an overlay we owned. Three stock mechanisms in
series:

  1. SDL's MFi joystick backend accepts ANY GCController that has a
     physicalInputProfile (build-visionos/_deps/sdl2-src/src/joystick/iphoneos/
     SDL_mfijoystick.m:488-492 -- stock, unpatched). A PS VR2 Sense has one, so
     each unit of the pair is ALSO an ordinary SDL game controller.
  2. LUS hands every SDL gamepad it finds to port 0
     (ConnectedPhysicalDeviceManager::RefreshConnectedSDLGamepads, this file:
     the loop ignores instance ids for ports 1..3 only).
  3. SoH's default mapping binds RIGHTX/RIGHTY to BTN_CLEFT / BTN_CRIGHT /
     BTN_CUP / BTN_CDOWN at the global 25% axis threshold
     (libultraship/src/libultraship/controller/controldevice/controller/mapping/
     ControllerDefaultMappings.cpp:86-102).

So the Sense pair played the game TWICE: once through overlay 0047's six-input
taxonomy, and once as an anonymous gamepad whose right stick is the C-pad.
"Sometimes" is exactly right -- it depended on which unit SDL enumerated and
whether its stick crossed 25%.

WHY OVERLAY 0039'S MASK CANNOT CLOSE IT, which is the part worth recording. 0039
clears the C bits a right-stick deflection produced, and it looks like the
natural home for this. It is not, for three independent reasons:

  * SCALE. 0039 tests the raw pad axis against 21 = 25% of MAX_AXIS_RANGE 85,
    because that is the threshold the LUS mapping uses on its own axis scale.
    The pad's own stick bytes are +/-127. The two thresholds are not the same
    deflection, so a mask written against one cannot exactly cancel bits set
    against the other.
  * PROVENANCE. rev5 made the mask exempt Sense-produced bits deliberately (a
    Sense C-left must survive a simultaneous stick-left). A C bit the SDL path
    raised FROM a Sense stick is Sense-produced by every test 0039 can make, so
    the exemption protects the very bits we want gone.
  * REACH. 0039 is gated on `gSohVRMode != 0 && flat_latch == 0`, i.e. VR
    gameplay only. Menus, the pause screen and the ocarina snapshot
    (Audio_GetOcaInput takes its own PadMgr_RequestPadData) all see the raw C
    bits. the user's report is "STILL sometimes", and those are the sometimes.

THE FIX IS AT THE SOURCE: the pair never becomes an SDL gamepad at all. One test
immediately after SDL_IsGameController, under __IOS__, asking the shell whether
this device index names a SPATIAL controller. The shell's predicate
(SohSense_IsSpatialControllerName, app/ios/SohSense.m) matches against the set of
GCController.vendorNames that GameController itself reported as
GCProductCategorySpatialController, which is exact, and falls back to a
conservative substring ("Sense", "PlayStation VR2") for the one ordering we
cannot rule out -- an enumeration that runs before any connect notification has
landed. SDL_JoystickNameForIndex returns GCController.vendorName verbatim on this
platform, which is what makes a name a sufficient identity here.

WHAT MUST KEEP WORKING, and is asserted rather than assumed: a real gamepad.
Nothing about this test can reach a device GameController does not call spatial,
so an MFi/Xbox/DualSense pad enumerates, opens, maps and plays exactly as before
-- in VR and everywhere else. The suite asserts the PREDICATE directly (`vr
sensename NAME`), because the simulator has no controller to enumerate, and
reports the live counts from `vr sdlpads`.

The skip is COUNTED (SohSense_NoteSdlSpatialSkip) so "the pair was excluded" is a
number in `vr sdlpads` rather than an inference from silence, and logged once per
device so a headset log says which name was skipped.

__IOS__-only, and the whole block is a no-op on every desktop build.

rev2 (VR R18 part B): THE iPHONE BUILD DID NOT LINK. __IOS__ is defined for the
iPhone build too (overlay 0022 flips CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME to "iOS" for visionOS, so
both platforms take every iOS path), but SohSense.m -- which DEFINES
SohSense_IsSpatialControllerName and SohSense_NoteSdlSpatialSkip -- is compiled
only under SOH_VISIONOS (soh/CMakeLists.txt: the visionOS block beside
SohImmersive.m and SohVrPhys.c). So rev1 made libultraship reference two symbols
the iPhone link has no definition for, and scripts/build-ios.sh has failed at the
link step ever since. A release ships both IPAs, so this blocked the release.
The guard is TARGET_OS_VISION rather than SOH_VISIONOS because SOH_VISIONOS is a
soh-target define and this file is in libultraship, which never sees it;
TARGET_OS_VISION comes from the SDK's own TargetConditionals.h (1 on xros, 0 on
iphoneos) and is therefore exactly as reliable as the SDK. There is nothing to
stub on iPhone: a PS VR2 Sense pair is a visionOS-only input path, so on iPhone
the vanilla enumeration is what is wanted, byte for byte.

